That's starting to change.

1854
01:16:17.734 --> 01:16:20.130
It's starting to change but there's a lot

1855
01:16:20.130 --> 01:16:22.023
of stuff out there that's,

1856
01:16:24.330 --> 01:16:25.830
I don't know is effective or not,

1857
01:16:25.830 --> 01:16:27.510
there's a lot of stuff out there.

1858
01:16:27.510 --> 01:16:29.320
In the end it's gotta be something,

1859
01:16:29.320 --> 01:16:30.640
what is the best workout,

1860
01:16:30.640 --> 01:16:32.430
what is the best nutrition plan?

1861
01:16:32.430 --> 01:16:34.762
It's the one you can do consistently.

1862
01:16:34.762 --> 01:16:37.450
That's what you gotta go for.

1863
01:16:37.450 --> 01:16:39.700
It's consistently that gives you the results.

1864
01:16:40.584 --> 01:16:43.656
Let's go back to forgiveness for a minute.

1865
01:16:43.656 --> 01:16:46.620
I think that's an important piece in all of this.

1866
01:16:46.620 --> 01:16:49.156
So how do you think about and practice that?

1867
01:16:49.156 --> 01:16:51.320
Well forgiveness, there's two forms,

1868
01:16:51.320 --> 01:16:53.670
right, forgiving others and forgiving yourself.

1869
01:16:55.400 --> 01:16:57.750
The novel that I wrote, "Rebirth",

1870
01:16:57.750 --> 01:16:58.670
that's about forgiveness

1871
01:16:58.670 --> 01:17:00.830
but that's about forgiving my father after he died.

1872
01:17:00.830 --> 01:17:05.100
The whole journey while I walked a pale pilgrimage in Spain

1873
01:17:05.100 --> 01:17:07.520
and the result of that pilgrimage was I learned

1874
01:17:07.520 --> 01:17:08.380
how to forgive him.

1875
01:17:08.380 --> 01:17:10.410
Right, like 550 miles?

1876
01:17:10.410 --> 01:17:12.053
Yeah, Camino de Santiago.

1877
01:17:12.954 --> 01:17:14.270
And you know what it was?

1878
01:17:14.270 --> 01:17:18.020
In the end it was just realizing his humanity.

1879
01:17:18.020 --> 01:17:19.560
He was a human being, man.

1880
01:17:19.560 --> 01:17:23.400
You can't hold humans to do the criteria of god's,

1881
01:17:23.400 --> 01:17:25.630
but we seem to do that.

1882
01:17:25.630 --> 01:17:28.229
And this really, his humanity.

1883
01:17:28.229 --> 01:17:30.110
When you realize someone's humanity,

1884
01:17:30.110 --> 01:17:31.940
it's very easy to forgive.

1885
01:17:31.940 --> 01:17:32.960
But in order to do that

1886
01:17:32.960 --> 01:17:37.960
you have to transcend your childhood lens

1887
01:17:38.580 --> 01:17:39.940
on your parents, right,

1888
01:17:39.940 --> 01:17:42.940
and shift that perspective to see it

1889
01:17:42.940 --> 01:17:45.025
through the eyes of the parents.

1890
01:17:45.025 --> 01:17:47.180
Or to see the eyes of a human being looking

1891
01:17:47.180 --> 01:17:48.610
at another human being.

1892
01:17:48.610 --> 01:17:49.980
You can never completely remove that,

1893
01:17:49.980 --> 01:17:50.813
right, the parent.

1894
01:17:50.813 --> 01:17:53.125
But if you can just do human being to another human being,

1895
01:17:53.125 --> 01:17:57.563
that right away shifts it and you see their struggles,

1896
01:17:58.728 --> 01:18:00.700
even faulty decision-making or whatever,

1897
01:18:00.700 --> 01:18:02.120
but their struggles.

1898
01:18:02.120 --> 01:18:03.818
When you understand that,

1899
01:18:03.818 --> 01:18:06.320
forgiveness actually comes naturally

1900
01:18:06.320 --> 01:18:07.490
because you can't help it.

1901
01:18:07.490 --> 01:18:09.100
You kind of understand.

1902
01:18:09.100 --> 01:18:10.970
You don't have to accept it.

1903
01:18:10.970 --> 01:18:12.540
You don't have to say yes, I agree.

1904
01:18:12.540 --> 01:18:14.550
I'm glad you were that way.

1905
01:18:14.550 --> 01:18:16.800
And I'm glad you did X, Y, and Z.

1906
01:18:16.800 --> 01:18:19.282
Because forgiveness ultimately is freeing yourself.

1907
01:18:19.282 --> 01:18:22.600
Yeah, I think we look at it as a two-way street.

1908
01:18:22.600 --> 01:18:25.390
I'll forgive you when you ask me for forgiveness,

1909
01:18:25.390 --> 01:18:28.320
or you admit what you did and how you wronged me.

1910
01:18:28.320 --> 01:18:31.810
But ultimately, the person who's carrying the resentment

1911
01:18:31.810 --> 01:18:36.410
or the anger or that pain is the one who is suffering.

1912
01:18:36.410 --> 01:18:37.970
The one who is the object

1913
01:18:37.970 --> 01:18:42.415
of being not forgiven is often even unaware.

1914
01:18:42.415 --> 01:18:43.950
That's our irony, right.

1915
01:18:43.950 --> 01:18:45.760
So it's a self-inflicted harm.

1916
01:18:45.760 --> 01:18:46.810
I was at an event,

1917
01:18:46.810 --> 01:18:47.907
a thing last night.

1918
01:18:47.907 --> 01:18:51.610
It's another little group thing that I do that was created

1919
01:18:51.610 --> 01:18:54.320
by this organization called the Nantucket Project.

1920
01:18:54.320 --> 01:18:56.260
And they created these neighborhood,

1921
01:18:56.260 --> 01:18:58.579
they're called neighborhood projects where little,

1922
01:18:58.579 --> 01:19:00.440
small groups of people get together

1923
01:19:00.440 --> 01:19:03.109
and they watch a short film

1924
01:19:03.109 --> 01:19:05.480
that the Nantucket Project produces,

1925
01:19:05.480 --> 01:19:08.640
and then a discussion ensues.

1926
01:19:08.640 --> 01:19:09.930
Kind of like to create

1927
01:19:12.970 --> 01:19:15.370
greater community in our neighborhoods.

1928
01:19:15.370 --> 01:19:18.230
And the movie that we watched last night

1929
01:19:18.230 --> 01:19:22.510
in this group setting was a short documentary about

1930
01:19:22.510 --> 01:19:26.400
the genocide in Rwanda and how the president

1931
01:19:26.400 --> 01:19:28.600
of Rwanda was faced with this

1932
01:19:30.750 --> 01:19:33.700
prospect of how to repair his country in the aftermath

1933
01:19:33.700 --> 01:19:35.860
of this horrible tragedy.

1934
01:19:35.860 --> 01:19:39.210
And the story is told from his perspective

1935
01:19:39.210 --> 01:19:43.110
and also through the perspective of a perpetrator

1936
01:19:43.110 --> 01:19:43.943
of the genocide,

1937
01:19:43.943 --> 01:19:45.230
somebody who killed a lot of people,

1938
01:19:45.230 --> 01:19:48.380
and a woman whose family had been killed.

1939
01:19:48.380 --> 01:19:50.050
And ultimately, it's a story about

1940
01:19:50.050 --> 01:19:53.530
how these two people forgive each other.

1941
01:19:53.530 --> 01:19:54.363
Wow.

1942
01:19:54.363 --> 01:19:56.810
And it's incredibly inspiring and it left me thinking

1943
01:19:56.810 --> 01:20:00.610
about the incredible exponential power of forgiveness.

1944
01:20:00.610 --> 01:20:03.130
I think there's something beautifully transcendent

1945
01:20:03.130 --> 01:20:04.770
and unique about forgiveness

1946
01:20:04.770 --> 01:20:08.230
in that it holds this potential energy capacity

1947
01:20:09.570 --> 01:20:12.130
to be so incredibly transformative.

1948
01:20:12.130 --> 01:20:13.630
Maybe it's because it's rare.

1949
01:20:13.630 --> 01:20:15.730
I don't know what it is about it uniquely,

1950
01:20:17.072 --> 01:20:18.863
but to see that,

1951
01:20:19.767 --> 01:20:22.150
it's impossible not to be moved.

1952
01:20:22.150 --> 01:20:24.220
And it leaves you thinking about

1953
01:20:26.660 --> 01:20:30.200
the people in our own lives that we refuse to forgive

1954
01:20:30.200 --> 01:20:33.050
or the resentments that we hold on to about

1955
01:20:33.050 --> 01:20:35.850
how we've been wronged and the pain that that creates

1956
01:20:35.850 --> 01:20:39.340
and the impediments to moving forward that constructs

1957
01:20:39.340 --> 01:20:40.630
in our lives.

1958
01:20:40.630 --> 01:20:42.223
And also,

1959
01:20:44.620 --> 01:20:46.350
about the people that we've harmed

1960
01:20:46.350 --> 01:20:49.230
and thinking more deeply about people who might be out

1961
01:20:49.230 --> 01:20:51.640
in the world carrying that kind of pain over things

1962
01:20:51.640 --> 01:20:52.763
that we might've done.

1963
01:20:54.090 --> 01:20:55.605
Yeah.

1964
01:20:55.605 --> 01:20:58.510
And then forgiving ourselves, right.

1965
01:20:58.510 --> 01:21:01.050
To repeat and loop the mantra,

1966
01:21:01.050 --> 01:21:02.640
I love myself,

1967
01:21:02.640 --> 01:21:05.390
I think self-forgiveness is a subset of that.

1968
01:21:05.390 --> 01:21:07.810
Yeah, that's actually the other part of forgiveness,

1969
01:21:07.810 --> 01:21:09.580
right, self-forgiveness.

1970
01:21:09.580 --> 01:21:13.150
For me, what I've learned is start with the self first.

1971
01:21:13.150 --> 01:21:14.060
You work on the self,

1972
01:21:14.060 --> 01:21:15.770
the rest actually gets easier.

1973
01:21:15.770 --> 01:21:17.833
The rest just naturally starts to work.

1974
01:21:17.833 --> 01:21:19.850
And the same thing with here was like in this book,

1975
01:21:19.850 --> 01:21:22.067
I have a practice that I do in forgiving myself

1976
01:21:22.067 --> 01:21:24.720
and it actually works beautifully.

1977
01:21:24.720 --> 01:21:26.630
And it's a very practical practice.

1978
01:21:26.630 --> 01:21:28.040
Like everything, it's practical,

1979
01:21:28.040 --> 01:21:30.509
you can do it and you will notice the shift.

1980
01:21:30.509 --> 01:21:33.290
And what I learned was I didn't have that

1981
01:21:33.290 --> 01:21:34.240
in the original version.

1982
01:21:34.240 --> 01:21:35.390
What I've learned is,

1983
01:21:35.390 --> 01:21:37.463
if you're gonna make a commitment to love yourself,

1984
01:21:37.463 --> 01:21:39.203
you can do this practice.

1985
01:21:40.741 --> 01:21:43.010
You can't leave the past behind but leave the shackles

1986
01:21:43.010 --> 01:21:44.510
of the past behind.

1987
01:21:44.510 --> 01:21:45.343
And what you do is you forgive yourself

1988
01:21:45.343 --> 01:21:47.720
and then you start to love yourself.

1989
01:21:47.720 --> 01:21:49.440
That's almost like the step-by-step.

1990
01:21:49.440 --> 01:21:52.641
And it makes a huge difference.

1991
01:21:52.641 --> 01:21:54.858
(dramatic swooshing)

1992
01:21:54.858 --> 01:21:57.690
Yeah, it's wild.

1993
01:21:57.690 --> 01:22:00.120
You mentioned that there's a lot of history

1994
01:22:00.120 --> 01:22:05.120 line:15%
behind why outdoor spaces have been considered off limits

1995
01:22:05.180 --> 01:22:06.513 line:15%
to people of color.

1996
01:22:08.780 --> 01:22:12.270
It's sickening and disheartening to hear that.

1997
01:22:12.270 --> 01:22:13.722
I believe it to be true.

1998
01:22:13.722 --> 01:22:15.850
But I can tell you that I haven't spent a lot

1999
01:22:15.850 --> 01:22:18.110
of time thinking about why that might be.

2000
01:22:18.110 --> 01:22:21.100
Well, if you think about how the

2001
01:22:22.150 --> 01:22:25.540
national parks were formulated

2002
01:22:25.540 --> 01:22:30.000
and how that land was stolen from native populations

2003
01:22:31.430 --> 01:22:35.771
without regard to any of their history or their domiciles,

2004
01:22:35.771 --> 01:22:39.320
I mean, that's just one part of that.

2005
01:22:39.320 --> 01:22:41.253
And I also think of how,

2006
01:22:43.810 --> 01:22:45.549
just land in the US,

2007
01:22:45.549 --> 01:22:46.670
(Mirna chuckling)

2008
01:22:46.670 --> 01:22:49.520
ownership of land is very, very white.

2009
01:22:49.520 --> 01:22:53.503
Ownership of any sort of real estate or land is very white.

2010
01:22:54.580 --> 01:22:56.430
And so

2011
01:22:57.580 --> 01:22:59.886
when someone like me appears,

2012
01:22:59.886 --> 01:23:02.260
like, I look like I don't belong

2013
01:23:02.260 --> 01:23:05.270
because the ownership of land has been traditionally white.

2014
01:23:05.270 --> 01:23:06.520
And so, I mean,

2015
01:23:06.520 --> 01:23:08.020
there's the government,

2016
01:23:08.020 --> 01:23:13.020
there are private entities that are also responsible

2017
01:23:13.370 --> 01:23:17.820
for this sort of white washing of land in the US.

2018
01:23:17.820 --> 01:23:20.940
And then there's white supremacy,

2019
01:23:20.940 --> 01:23:22.390
white supremacist ideology

2020
01:23:25.629 --> 01:23:28.620
that has it that black people don't belong

2021
01:23:28.620 --> 01:23:31.010
and we are seen as nuisances

2022
01:23:31.010 --> 01:23:32.940
in many different types of spaces.

2023
01:23:32.940 --> 01:23:35.480
And there's research on that too

2024
01:23:35.480 --> 01:23:38.800
on blacks is a nuisance

2025
01:23:38.800 --> 01:23:41.666
in public spaces.

2026
01:23:41.666 --> 01:23:42.970
(Rich sighing)

2027
01:23:42.970 --> 01:23:44.610
It's so heavy.
It's really heavy.

2028
01:23:44.610 --> 01:23:46.760
You see, I'm smiling because it's so heavy.

2029
01:23:48.076 --> 01:23:51.805
And to have to think about that constantly,

2030
01:23:51.805 --> 01:23:53.755
and I do think about it constantly,

2031
01:23:53.755 --> 01:23:56.340
look, every second that I'm on a trail.

2032
01:23:56.340 --> 01:23:58.840
I mean, I may be smiling and I may be gracious and

2033
01:24:00.370 --> 01:24:02.210
affable, but I am always,

2034
01:24:02.210 --> 01:24:07.210
always thinking about whether or not people think I belong?

2035
01:24:07.390 --> 01:24:11.500
And is somebody gonna ask me a dumb ass question

2036
01:24:11.500 --> 01:24:13.220
or are they gonna say something stupid

2037
01:24:13.220 --> 01:24:16.030
or are they going to make me feel as though I'm not welcome?

2038
01:24:16.030 --> 01:24:20.040
I'm always thinking of that which is why I'm so effusive

2039
01:24:20.040 --> 01:24:22.650
with my cheer because I have to be.

2040
01:24:22.650 --> 01:24:26.080
Yeah, you feel like part of that is a defense mechanism

2041
01:24:26.080 --> 01:24:29.810
to put other people at ease which makes you feel more safe.

2042
01:24:29.810 --> 01:24:30.920
Absolutely. Yeah.

2043
01:24:30.920 --> 01:24:34.260
I mean, that's the story of every black runner.

2044
01:24:34.260 --> 01:24:39.260
Every single person who goes out and runs who is black,

2045
01:24:40.560 --> 01:24:41.460
we have to signal.

2046
01:24:42.720 --> 01:24:44.060
There's a term for it.

2047
01:24:44.060 --> 01:24:45.303
It's called signaling,

2048
01:24:45.303 --> 01:24:48.670
to signal to other people that we are safe to be around

2049
01:24:48.670 --> 01:24:49.983
and we are not a threat.

2050
01:24:51.570 --> 01:24:53.980
Yeah, you hear stories of black runners

2051
01:24:53.980 --> 01:24:56.230
who will wear a sweatshirt or a

2052
01:24:56.230 --> 01:24:57.063
T-shirt that has
From their college.

2053
01:24:57.063 --> 01:24:59.560
a fancy college name on it or something like that

2054
01:24:59.560 --> 01:25:02.520
to make white people feel like this person is not a threat.

2055
01:25:02.520 --> 01:25:03.360
It doesn't matter.

2056
01:25:03.360 --> 01:25:05.740
It doesn't matter what you put on.

2057
01:25:05.740 --> 01:25:09.299
Again, I'm always decked out in a latest gear, right.

2058
01:25:09.299 --> 01:25:10.200
(Mirna chuckling)

2059
01:25:10.200 --> 01:25:11.743
I wear bright colors.

2060
01:25:13.600 --> 01:25:16.583
I've got my running cap and my trucker cap on,

2061
01:25:17.770 --> 01:25:20.830
a race shirt or something that signals to people

2062
01:25:20.830 --> 01:25:21.763
that I'm a runner.

2063
01:25:22.620 --> 01:25:25.460
When I still lived in Georgia I was running

2064
01:25:25.460 --> 01:25:27.860
down my own street

2065
01:25:29.750 --> 01:25:33.270
and I was two miles,

2066
01:25:33.270 --> 01:25:35.437
I had done 14 miles

2067
01:25:35.437 --> 01:25:37.950
and so I was finishing up the last two miles

2068
01:25:37.950 --> 01:25:40.180
to do a 16 miler.

2069
01:25:40.180 --> 01:25:44.470
On my way back home a woman in a white SUV was coming

2070
01:25:44.470 --> 01:25:48.480
in the opposite direction and stops about a 1/4

2071
01:25:48.480 --> 01:25:49.630
of a mile away from me.

2072
01:25:50.600 --> 01:25:53.060
I'm slow, so it takes me a long time to do a 1/4 of a mile.

2073
01:25:53.060 --> 01:25:55.490
So I was still pretty far away from her.

2074
01:25:55.490 --> 01:25:56.323
So she stops,

2075
01:25:56.323 --> 01:25:57.156
she takes out her phone.

2076
01:25:57.156 --> 01:25:58.120
She's looking at me.

2077
01:25:58.120 --> 01:25:59.180
She's talking on her phone,

2078
01:25:59.180 --> 01:26:00.013
looking at me,

2079
01:26:00.013 --> 01:26:00.846
talking to her phone.

2080
01:26:00.846 --> 01:26:03.696
Again, I am really decked out.

2081
01:26:03.696 --> 01:26:04.925
I mean, sweaty,

2082
01:26:04.925 --> 01:26:05.960
(Mirna chuckling)

2083
01:26:05.960 --> 01:26:08.083
but really decked out in all my running gear.

2084
01:26:10.440 --> 01:26:13.780
And then she gets off the phone and slowly rolls by me

2085
01:26:13.780 --> 01:26:16.010
and looks at me and I wave,

2086
01:26:16.010 --> 01:26:17.423
I'm smiling, hey,

2087
01:26:18.620 --> 01:26:20.210
wondering

2088
01:26:20.210 --> 01:26:21.489
what the hell she was doing.
And you do that on purpose

2089
01:26:21.489 --> 01:26:23.060
because you know?
On purpose.

2090
01:26:23.060 --> 01:26:24.374
Yeah, okay.

2091
01:26:24.374 --> 01:26:29.080
And not even five minutes later a cop car rolls by

2092
01:26:29.080 --> 01:26:31.350
from that same direction that she had been going

2093
01:26:31.350 --> 01:26:32.910
and they slowed down,

2094
01:26:32.910 --> 01:26:33.743
rolled down the window,

2095
01:26:33.743 --> 01:26:34.580
look at me.

2096
01:26:34.580 --> 01:26:36.090
I wave.

2097
01:26:36.090 --> 01:26:36.923
In my head I'm, like,

2098
01:26:36.923 --> 01:26:37.840
what the fuck?

2099
01:26:37.840 --> 01:26:38.980
Right.

2100
01:26:38.980 --> 01:26:42.930
And that cop rolls away and then another one comes

2101
01:26:42.930 --> 01:26:46.623
from the opposite direction, slows down,

2102
01:26:48.090 --> 01:26:52.070
rolls her window down and then I wave again,

2103
01:26:52.070 --> 01:26:54.593
hey, have a good day.

2104
01:26:55.749 --> 01:26:57.043
This is in your neighborhood.

2105
01:26:57.043 --> 01:26:59.173
This is two miles away from my house.

2106
01:27:00.166 --> 01:27:02.344
(Mirna chuckling)

2107
01:27:02.344 --> 01:27:03.367
And in my head, I'm like,

2108
01:27:03.367 --> 01:27:06.530
I'm just running. I am running.

2109
01:27:06.530 --> 01:27:08.770
I did have a walking stick that I carried

2110
01:27:08.770 --> 01:27:11.380
because there were lots of dogs.

2111
01:27:11.380 --> 01:27:14.360
There were no leash laws where I used to live.

2112
01:27:14.360 --> 01:27:15.690
And so there were dogs everywhere

2113
01:27:15.690 --> 01:27:18.080
and so I would have to bang the stick on the ground

2114
01:27:18.080 --> 01:27:19.623
to get the dogs away from me.

2115
01:27:20.890 --> 01:27:22.890
And so,

2116
01:27:22.890 --> 01:27:25.867
but it was a very fancy carved walking stick.

